# UTC Datetime: Always

RULE: All datetime objects must be timezone-aware UTC. Never use naive datetimes or deprecated utcnow().
WHY: Naive datetimes cause silent DST bugs, incorrect comparisons, and partition misalignment in GCS time-series data.
CODEX: 06-coding-standards/README.md

DO (Python):
  from datetime import datetime, timezone
  now = datetime.now(timezone.utc)
  ts = datetime(2026, 1, 1, tzinfo=timezone.utc)

NEVER (Python):
  datetime.now()         # naive — no timezone
  datetime.utcnow()      # deprecated since Python 3.12
  datetime.today()       # naive
  timezone-naive timestamps in any GCS schema or event metadata

## Shell Scripts: No Timestamp-Based Freshness Checks

RULE: Shell scripts MUST NOT use filesystem mtime to decide whether to re-run steps.
WHY: `-nt`, `-mtime`, and `stat` mtime comparisons use the local system clock timezone. A file created at 17:00 IST and compared on a UK machine running UTC appears "in the future" or "stale" incorrectly. This caused real setup failures for developers in India vs UK.

NEVER (shell):
  [ "pyproject.toml" -nt "uv.lock" ]         # local mtime — timezone-broken
  find . -mtime +1 -name "*.stamp" -delete   # 24h periods from local midnight
  touch .setup-stamp && check if -nt         # stamp files for freshness

DO INSTEAD (shell):
  uv lock --check            # semantic: exits 1 if lock is out of sync
  uv pip install (idempotent) # always re-run; uv is fast and skips unchanged deps
  hash/checksum comparison   # content-based, not time-based
